[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I inspected OSPF on the three core endpoints (csw1, csw2, r1). All adjacencies are presently stable and Full. On r1, “show ip ospf neighbor” shows both core links up Full/DR to csw1 (eth0: 10.0.0.33/30) and csw2 (eth1: 10.0.0.37/30) with clean neighbor queues (RXmtL/RqstL/DBsmL all 0). The same is mirrored on csw1 and csw2 toward r1. Interface detail confirms consistent timers (Hello 10s/Dead 40s), no MTU mismatches, and stable DR/BDR roles. Interface counters on r1 eth0/eth1 and csw1/csw2 WAN-facing ports show no errors or drops.

Neighbor “Up Time” values across the core (≈3–4 minutes when checked) and OSPF logs on csw1/csw2 (“Packet[DD]: Neighbor … Negotiation done”) indicate a one-off convergence shortly after 01:24, coinciding with LACP bond aggregation events on the csw1/csw2 interconnect (“bond0 old speed: 10000 new speed: 20000”). That initial bring-up produces the expected six OSPF state transitions (Down→Init→ExStart→Exchange→Loading→Full) seen in neighbor detail, which can trigger “flap” alerts if monitoring lacks startup suppression. There is no evidence of repeated neighbor resets, stuck states, or interface flaps.

Root cause: a transient convergence at service/link initialization triggered a false-positive “flapping” alert. Corrective fix: no restart is required. Recommend updating monitoring to suppress OSPF adjacency alerts during boot/convergence or when LACP aggregates form. If operations insists, do not bounce any core or WAN links—everything is healthy. 
=== END ===